          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How long does a common cold usually last?",
          "answer": "A common cold usually lasts about 7 to 10 days. The symptoms, such as a stuffy or runny nose, sneezing, and sore throat, typically peak around days two and three. (Source: \"Upper respiratory infection (common cold)\", general clinical reference.)\n\nA cough often lasts longer, sometimes two to three weeks. The reference says this is normal and does not by itself mean a bacterial infection or that treatment has failed. (Same source.)\n\nMucus that turns yellow or green is also part of the normal course, according to the same reference. Guidance on antibiotics (\"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship\") says antibiotics generally do not shorten a common cold. If symptoms seem to be getting worse rather than better, or you are concerned, talk to a clinician.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover when a cold would count as needing medical review, or specific self-care steps. A clinician can help with those questions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
